From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 03:38:04 +0200
Subject: Tile: read the map's PNG as ARGB on big-endian machines

The ground's tiles test each pixel as a Uint32 0xAARRGGBB (alpha mask
0xFF000000), but the PNG rows were always read as B, G, R, A bytes
(png_set_bgr), which is that value only on a little-endian machine. On
big-endian (the PS3) the alpha test read the blue byte, so most of the
ground counted as empty: every character was "probably misplaced" and fell
into the water at the start, and the match ended at once. Big-endian now
reads A, R, G, B (png_set_swap_alpha).

Forwarded: not-needed (worth offering upstream)

diff --git a/src/map/tile.cpp b/src/map/tile.cpp
--- a/src/map/tile.cpp
+++ b/src/map/tile.cpp
@@ -457,8 +457,15 @@ bool Tile::LoadImage(const std::string& filename,
   if (depth == 16)
     png_set_strip_16(png_ptr);
 
-  // Find the proper mapping of channels
+  // Find the proper mapping of channels: the tiles read each pixel as a Uint32 0xAARRGGBB (alpha mask
+  // 0xFF000000), so the bytes in memory are B, G, R, A on a little-endian machine and A, R, G, B on a
+  // big-endian one. With BGRA there (as before), the alpha test read the blue byte: on the PS3 most of
+  // the ground counted as empty and every character fell into the water at the start.
+#if SDL_BYTEORDER == SDL_BIG_ENDIAN
+  png_set_swap_alpha(png_ptr);
+#else
   png_set_bgr(png_ptr);
+#endif
 
   FreeMem();
   m_alpha_threshold = alpha_threshold;
